stream
␀␀^jumb␀␀␀jumdc2pa␀␀€␀␀ª␀8›qc2pa␀␀␀8jumb␀␀␀Gjumdc2ma␀␀€␀␀ª␀8›qurn:uuid:229df72f-1bd9-4313-9601-24daeab2ab61␀␀␀†jumb␀␀␀)jumdc2as␀␀€␀␀ª␀8›qc2pa.assertions␀␀␀jumb␀␀␀)jumdcbor␀␀€␀␀ª␀8›qc2pa.actions.v2␀␀␀Öcbor¡gactionsƒ¤factionlc2pa.createdqdigitalSourceTypex>http://cv.iptc.org/newscodes/digitalsourcetype/digitalCreationmsoftwareAgent¢dnameeLollygversiong1.225.0dwhent2026-09-26T21:33:11Z¤factionvc2pa.color_adjustmentskdescriptionx1Snapped colours to the brand palette (66 colours)msoftwareAgent¢dnameeLollygversiong1.225.0dwhent2026-09-26T21:33:11Z¤factionnc2pa.convertedkdescriptionoRendered to PDFmsoftwareAgent¢dnameeLollygversiong1.225.0dwhent2026-09-26T21:33:11Z␀␀␀®jumb␀␀␀(jumdcbor␀␀€␀␀ª␀8›qc2pa.hash.data␀␀␀␀~cbor¥jexclusions¢estart␀¦Mflength^dnamenjumbf manifestcalgfsha256dhashX ÂªUßçöÛò)Œb%’†}ÜÒ±?úä}¯w6÷3®’/½cpadH␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ jumb␀␀␀,jumdcbor␀␀€␀␀ª␀8›qtools.lolly.export␀␀␀lcborªdtoolfDesignftoolIdfdesignktoolVersionf1.37.1fformatcpdfgsurfacecwebfenginelChromium 153bosemacOSddatex2026-09-26T21:33:11.266Zjdimensionsn794 Ã— 1123 pxfinputs¯leditingRangecsdrjbackgroundg#dfe5e2ldocumentUnitbmmkdocumentDpic300jtransitioneslidekautoAdvanceefalsennarrationSpeeda1qnarrationLeadInMsc400onarrationTailMsc600xshowCaptionsWhenPresentingefalsejprojectFpsb30ltextDocumenty¢{"version":1,"stories":[{"version":1,"id":"cover-title","revision":0,"source":"Shoulders\nof giants","breaks":[{"start":9,"length":1,"kind":"paragraph"}],"spans":[],"inlines":[],"frameIds":["t-3"],"paragraphs":[{"start":0,"end":9,"id":"cover-title-p0","style":"cover-title"},{"start":10,"end":19,"id":"cover-title-p1","style":"cover-title"}]},{"version":1,"id":"cover-dek","revision":0,"source":"Lolly is a few years of work standing on several decades of theirs.","breaks":[],"spans":[],"inlines":[],"frameIds":["t-4"],"paragraphs":[{"start":0,"end":67,"id":"cover-dek-p0","style":"cover-dek"}]},{"version":1,"id":"cover-epigraph","revision":0,"source":"If I have seen further it is by standing on the shoulders of giants.\nIsaac Newton, in a letter to Robert Hooke, 1675","breaks":[{"start":68,"length":1,"kind":"paragraph"}],"spans":[],"inlines":[],"frameIds":["t-29"],"paragraphs":[{"start":0,"end":68,"id":"cover-epigraph-p0","style":"cover-quote"},{"start":69,"end":116,"id":"cover-epigraph-p1","style":"cover-by"}]},{"version":1,"id":"lede","revision":0,"source":"Newton did not say where he found that line. It was already five centuries old: in 1159 John of Salisbury wrote that Bernard of Chartres used to say we are like dwarfs sitting on the shoulders of giants, and it took later scholars to trace the thread back that far. Credit is easy to lose along the way. The Lolly project strives to honour attribution, provenance and all the legacies it builds on.","breaks":[],"spans":[{"start":0,"end":1,"character":{"color":"#30ba78","weight":800}}],"inlines":[],"frameIds":["t-37"],"paragraphs":[{"start":0,"end":398,"id":"lede-p0","style":"lede"}]},{"version":1,"id":"opening","revision":0,"source":"If you are new to how software is made, start here: almost nothing in the apps you use every day was written from scratch by the company whose name is on the box. It is assembled from parts that thousands of people wrote, tested and gave away, some of them decades ago, most of them strangers to each other. Lolly is a few years of work standing on several decades of theirs.\nLolly signs what it makes; this page credits what it is made from. It tells the story in order, from the operating system at the bottom to the newest models at the top, and then lists everyone else the story had no room for. The legal record, with every licence in full, ships inside every copy of the app and is linked at the very end.","breaks":[{"start":375,"length":1,"kind":"paragraph"}],"spans":[],"inlines":[],"frameIds":["t-38"],"paragraphs":[{"start":0,"end":375,"id":"opening-p0","style":"body-first"},{"start":376,"end":712,"id":"opening-p1","style":"body"}]},{"version":1,"id":"how-head","revision":0,"source":"How world-class software is really made","breaks":[],"spans":[],"inlines":[],"frameIds":["t-41"],"paragraphs":[{"start":0,"end":39,"id":"how-head-p0","style":"h2"}]},{"version":1,"id":"how","revision":0,"source":"Open source is a simple arrangement with large consequences. The authors publish their code under a licence that lets anyone read, use, change and share the code. Most of those licences ask one thing in return: that you say where the work came from. Some, called copyleft licences, ask more: if you share a changed version, you share it on the same terms, so the work stays open wherever it travels. A few ask nothing at all.\nIt sounds like charity, and mostly it is not. Companies pay engineers to work in the open because a shared foundation, maintained by everyone who depends on it, is cheaper and better than a private one that each of them would have to build alone. Volunteers give their evenings because building something useful beside people you respect is one of the more satisfying things a person can do. Most of the projects on this page are a mixture of both.\nThe scale is hard to picture. In 2024, economists at Harvard Business School estimated that writing the world's widely used open source software once more would cost about $4.15 billion, while its vaâ€¦qexportVisibleTextefalsemtransparentBgdtruelconvertPathsdtrue␀␀jumb␀␀␀'jumdc2cl␀␀€␀␀ª␀8›qc2pa.claim.v2␀␀␀Òcbor¦hdc:titlefDesignjinstanceIDx-urn:uuid:2a6724a7-5da7-4a7d-a96f-1c9de7cfea6atclaim_generator_info¢dnameeLollygversiong1.225.0rcreated_assertionsƒ¢curlx*self#jumbf=c2pa.assertions/c2pa.actions.v2dhashX hRÍù*”BûZ[®Sÿ%nÇØÓì{f‡="ñ%Sz¢curlx)self#jumbf=c2pa.assertions/c2pa.hash.datadhashX ³i ¢Ök†+«¦©D‰UÕ;ÍZAˆrC'qä¢curlx-self#jumbf=c2pa.assertions/tools.lolly.exportdhashX QœÙ:êFþ5«˜nkžQ$ÖïÔí|J”Ð‚”9ðI¡çdisignaturexself#jumbf=c2pa.signaturecalgfsha256␀␀bjumb␀␀␀(jumdc2cs␀␀€␀␀ª␀8›qc2pa.signature␀␀␀2cborÒ„Yá¢&!YØ0‚Ô0‚y 	Tiì¬ŽRù0
